  {
   "code": "PIPE_OK",
   "severity": "info",
   "ruleId": "PLUMBING_SIZE_GUIDANCE",
   "text": "2″ plumbing carries about 63 GPM suction / 84 GPM return within the velocity limits."
  },
  {
   "code": "DATA_QUALITY_FLAG",
   "severity": "info",
   "ruleId": "SWG_OUTPUT_HAIRCUT",
   "text": "Some models have disputed published output (badged below). Measured output generally matches published figures, so no derating is applied — but confirm with the manufacturer."
  },
  {
   "code": "SWG_FLOW_SWITCH",
   "severity": "info",
   "ruleId": "SWG_FLOW_SWITCH",
   "text": "If you run a variable-speed pump, keep the lowest speed above roughly 25 GPM (2″ tee). The flow switch reacts to water velocity, so too slow a speed stops chlorine production without any obvious fault."
  },
  {
   "code": "SWG_CYA_CELL_LIFE",
   "severity": "info",
   "ruleId": "SWG_CYA_CELL_LIFE",
   "text": "Hold CYA at 70–80 once you are running on salt. Higher CYA shields chlorine from sunlight, so the cell makes less to hold the same FC and lasts longer. Note the output percentage is a share of pump run time — change your schedule and you will need to re-tune it."
  },
  {
   "code": "CELL_LIFE",
   "severity": "info",
   "ruleId": "CELL_LIFE",
   "text": "Cell life is measured in generating hours — roughly 10,000 for OEM cells, nearer 7,000 for generics (some offshore cells as low as 3,000). At about 71% output the top pick spends most of the day idle, which is exactly why TFP says to oversize."
  },
  {
